/*
   Check if the updates below the tolerance of LookupOnChange expressions
   triggers the evaluation of its expression or not.

   To make this observation reproduce the following steps:
     1. Compile `lookup2.ple` and `lookup2.pst`
     2. Call the PLEXIL *test executive* with a debugger
        ```
        $ lldb $PLEXIL_HOME/bin/TestExec
        ```
     3. Set a breakpoint where the *StartCondition* is checked inside
        the `NodeImpl::getDestStateFromWaiting` method
        ```
        (lldb) break set -f NodeImpl.cc -l 1042
        ```
     4. Run the program with the compiled plan and script
        ```
        (lldb) run -p lookup2.plx -s lookup2.psx -d Debug.All.cfg
        ```
     5. At this point the *StartCondition* is going to be evaluated for the first time.
        If you follow step-by-step the evaluation. You can confirm that the *LookupOnChange*
        gets evaluated to its initial cached value `10` producing a `false` subexpression.
        The condition is evaluated even though the newest *lookup* value is not within the
        specified tolerance.
        ```
        (lldb) s
        ...
        (lldb) n
        ...
        (lldb) p <variable>
        ```
     6. If you skip the two next breakpoints, you get to the next *macro-step* and the second
        evaluation of the *StartCondition* even though the new globally cached value `10.5`
        is within the specified tolerance.
        ```
        (lldb) continue
        (lldb) continue
        (lldb) s
        ...
        (lldb) n
        ...
        (lldb) p <variable>
        ```
 */
Command pprint(...);
Real Lookup time;

lookup2: Concurrence
{
    Boolean TRUE = true;
    Exit Lookup(time) > 12;
    Repeat true;
    Start Lookup(time, 1) > 11 || TRUE;
    pprint("lookup2: ", Lookup(time));
}